SPEAKER NOTES / SHORT TALKING POINTS
Problem: Buildings use electricity for lighting, HVAC, equipment and other operations. Consumption dashboards describe use, but often don't help compare potential changes.
Solution: BUILDWISE accepts basic building data, computes EPI, and lets a user run simple what-if scenarios.
Sustainability: Lower electricity use may lower indirect emissions; the result depends on the grid emission factor. Financial results depend on tariff and costs.
Technical: HTML structures the page, CSS styles it responsively, and JavaScript validates inputs and performs calculations in the browser. No database or sensor connection is currently included.
Limitations: This is not a calibrated digital twin or professional audit. The selected reduction is an assumption entered by the user.
Future work: Use verified bills/monthly data, documented emission factors, CSV upload, stronger model calibration, and stakeholder testing.

LIKELY VIVA QUESTIONS & ANSWERS
1. What is a digital twin here?
A simplified digital representation of a building using entered attributes and energy data. This prototype is not a live sensor-connected twin.
2. Why call it “what-if”?
Users can change assumed measures and compare estimated outcomes before implementing them.
3. How is EPI calculated?
Annual electricity consumption in kWh divided by relevant floor area in m².
4. Does the site use real-time data?
No. Current prototype uses manual inputs; no sensor/IoT feed is connected.
5. How is energy saving calculated?
Baseline annual kWh multiplied by the assumed reduction percentage.
6. How is cost saving calculated?
Estimated kWh saved multiplied by the entered tariff per kWh.
7. How are avoided emissions estimated?
Estimated kWh saved multiplied by the entered grid emission factor.
8. What is simple payback?
Estimated implementation cost divided by estimated annual bill savings. It ignores maintenance, financing, incentives and future tariff changes.
9. Are the results guaranteed?
No. They are illustrative estimates and depend on the accuracy of assumptions and actual building conditions.
10. Why use JavaScript?
It runs calculations and updates the interface directly in the browser without a backend for this prototype.
11. Why no database?
The current demonstration is a front-end prototype. Persistent accounts/storage can be future work.
12. What are the limitations?
Simplified percentage model, manual inputs, no weather or equipment calibration, no live monitoring, and context-dependent emission factors.
13. What happens if input is invalid?
The profile form checks required fields and rejects invalid numeric values.
14. What makes it different from an energy dashboard?
It adds an interactive scenario-comparison layer rather than only displaying past/current consumption.
15. What future improvements are possible?
CSV upload, verified monthly data, regional emission factors, calibrated simulation, smart meter integration and user testing.
16. Why is this relevant to SDG 7?
It supports awareness and planning around energy efficiency and sustainable energy use; it does not itself deliver energy access.
17. What does “recommendation” mean in this prototype?
It provides a simple interpretation of the assumed payback; it is not an AI-optimized or professionally validated recommendation.
18. What did each member contribute?
Answer truthfully with your actual division of work. Do not claim a feature was individually built unless that is accurate.
19. How did you test it?
Tested the profile flow, calculations with illustrative inputs, scenario output, monthly chart and text export. Only claim tests you actually performed.
20. What is the next step before real deployment?
Validate the model with building bills and technical data, document factors and assumptions, test with building managers, and add secure data storage if needed.

